The characteristic histopathological feature of basal cell carcinoma is nuclear palisading. This is a hallmark of the disease and is caused by the accumulation of cells in a palisading pattern, typically due to a defect in the cell cycle regulation. 

Keratin pearls are more commonly associated with squamous cell carcinoma, psammoma bodies are seen in papillary thyroid carcinoma, and foam cells are associated with lipid disorders or certain types of tumors, not basal cell carcinoma.

