Interruption of the optic chiasm will lead to bitemporal hemianopia. This is because the optic chiasm is where the optic nerves from both eyes cross. If this structure is disrupted, the visual fields in both temporal regions (the sides of the visual field) are affected, resulting in bitemporal hemianopia. This condition is typically associated with conditions such as pituitary tumors or other lesions that compress the chiasm.

Binasal hemianopia refers to visual field defects in both nasal quadrants, which is more consistent with optic radiation lesions. Homonymous hemianopia involves visual field defects in both eyes in the same hemisphere, which is more consistent with optic chiasmal lesions. Normal vision is not a result of optic chiasm interruption.

