The reverse bevel incision is a common surgical technique used in periodontal procedures. It is designed to provide access to the alveolar crest, which is the bone that supports the teeth. This incision also allows for atraumatic reflection of the gingival margin, which is important for preserving the delicate gum tissue. Additionally, it facilitates the removal of infected tissue from the sulcus.

Option D, "All of the above," is the most accurate because all three functions—allowing atraumatic reflection of the gingival margin, removing infected tissue in the sulcus, and providing access to the alveolar crest—are associated with the reverse bevel incision.

